<% content_for :title, "CBT & Addiction | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="cbt-addiction-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="cbt-addiction-hero">
    <picture class="cbt-addiction-background">
      <source media="(max-width: 767px)"
              srcset="<%= asset_path('addiction_cbt_mobile.png') %>">
      <%= image_tag "addiction_cbt_desktop.png",
          alt: "",
          class: "cbt-addiction-background-image",
          fetchpriority: "high" %>
    </picture>

    <div class="cbt-addiction-hero-content">
      <p class="cbt-addiction-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / PERSPECTIVES / CBT
      </p>
      <h1>
        <span>ADDICTION</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>CBT LENS.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="cbt-addiction-intro">
        EXAMINE THE THOUGHT.<br>
        LOOK AT THE WHOLE FUCKING PICTURE.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 01 / OPENING -->
  <section class="ca-section ca-pink">
    <div class="ca-heading">
      <p class="ca-number">01 / ANOTHER WAY TO LOOK</p>
      <h2>WHAT'S<br>THE STORY<br>HERE?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ca-copy">
      <blockquote>
        “Your feelings are not facts!”
        <cite>— David D. Burns (attribution to verify)</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <p class="ca-lead">
        Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) examines how thoughts,
        emotions, and behaviors influence one another.
      </p>
      <p>
        Addiction isn't only about wanting alcohol. It's also about
        what you expect drinking to accomplish, the stories you tell
        yourself in the moment, and the habits reinforced by temporary
        relief. CBT asks you to examine those expectations against
        what actually happened—not to deny real pain or pretend that
        every feeling is a distortion.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 / THE PATTERN -->
  <section class="ca-section ca-yellow">
    <div class="ca-heading">
      <p class="ca-number">02 / THOUGHTS, FEELINGS & BEHAVIOR</p>
      <h2>LOOK AT<br>HOW IT<br>CONNECTS.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ca-copy">
      <p class="ca-lead">
        A thought can shape a feeling. A feeling can push a behavior.
        The result can make the same behavior more likely next time.
      </p>
      <div class="ca-grid">
        <div class="ca-box">
          <h3>THE SITUATION</h3>
          <p>Severe pain, a manic episode, or a disagreement with someone you care about.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ca-box">
          <h3>THE THOUGHT</h3>
          <p>“I can't stand this.” “Jackpot.” “They must hate me.”</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ca-box">
          <h3>THE FEELING</h3>
          <p>Desperation, excitement, anger, sadness, or an urgent need for relief.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ca-box">
          <h3>THE BEHAVIOR</h3>
          <p>Drinking for relief, impulsively buying alcohol, or sending messages you later regret.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        These patterns can become automatic. Alcohol may temporarily
        reduce discomfort (negative reinforcement) or produce pleasure
        (positive reinforcement), even when the consequences are harmful.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 / DISTORTIONS -->
  <section class="ca-section ca-purple">
    <div class="ca-heading">
      <p class="ca-number">03 / FAMILIAR THINKING PATTERNS</p>
      <h2>CATCH THE<br>SWEEPING<br>VERDICT.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ca-copy">
      <p class="ca-lead">
        Cognitive distortions can turn one difficult moment into
        a certainty about your future or somebody else's intentions.
      </p>
      <div class="ca-thoughts">
        <div>
          <h3>ALL-OR-NOTHING THINKING</h3>
          <p>During your First DUI, alcohol felt like your only answer to pain, even though other options existed or might become available.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>CATASTROPHIZING</h3>
          <p>Seeing an older cyclist while you were injured brought up the fear that you would be stuck that way forever.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>MIND READING & PERSONALIZATION</h3>
          <p>In Press Send, you sometimes assumed friends hated you or that their decisions were directed against you.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>ASSUMPTIONS & PREDICTIONS</h3>
          <p>Feeling rejected doesn't establish that you have been abandoned. Feeling desperate doesn't prove you have no alternatives.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Not every painful thought is inaccurate, and you don't identify
        all your reactions as emotional reasoning. CBT invites you to
        investigate each thought rather than assigning a label automatically.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 / EXAMINING THE EVIDENCE -->
  <section class="ca-section ca-black">
    <div class="ca-heading">
      <p class="ca-number">04 / EXAMINING THE EVIDENCE</p>
      <h2>DOES IT<br>ACTUALLY<br>HOLD UP?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ca-copy">
      <p class="ca-lead">
        A thought can be convincing without telling the whole story.
      </p>
      <div class="ca-stack">
        <div class="ca-stack-item">
          <span>01</span>
          <div><h3>NAME THE THOUGHT</h3><p>“Alcohol is the only thing that will help.”</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ca-stack-item">
          <span>02</span>
          <div><h3>LOOK AT THE EVIDENCE</h3><p>Your pain was real, and alcohol briefly relieved it. But it didn't treat the injury, and drinking while driving caused new harm.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ca-stack-item">
          <span>03</span>
          <div><h3>CONSIDER ANOTHER ACCOUNT</h3><p>“I'm in severe pain and need relief, but alcohol isn't my only possible response.”</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ca-stack-item">
          <span>04</span>
          <div><h3>IDENTIFY A USEFUL ACTION</h3><p>Seek appropriate support and make a plan before pain, craving, or impulsivity becomes a crisis.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 / YOUR STORIES -->
  <section class="ca-section ca-pink">
    <div class="ca-heading">
      <p class="ca-number">05 / THE ALCOHOL DIARIES</p>
      <h2>INCLUDE<br>THE WHOLE<br>EXPERIENCE.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ca-copy">
      <p class="ca-lead">
        Three different situations. Three different expectations.
        The same need to examine what happened before and after.
      </p>

      <h3>FIRST DUI / PAIN & RELIEF</h3>
      <p>
        Severe sciatica made your situation feel unbearable. You feared
        the pain would last forever and treated alcohol as your only solution.
        Its temporary relief reinforced drinking, so you drank faster in pursuit
        of more relief without considering the consequences. CBT distinguishes
        the reality of your pain from the all-or-nothing belief that drinking
        was your only response.
      </p>

      <h3>THIRD DUI / MANIA & IMPULSE</h3>
      <p>
        You knew you were manic and had already been drinking secretly.
        When the interlock didn't request more tests, your brain went haywire:
        <em>Jackpot</em> said drink; <em>No</em> was the sensible response you ignored.
        You weren't weighing risks or calculating control. You wanted alcohol.
        CBT helps identify that sequence, but challenging thoughts alone cannot
        account for mania's effects on judgment and impulse control. Earlier
        warning signs and support matter.
      </p>

      <h3>PRESS SEND / ASSUMPTIONS & RELATIONSHIPS</h3>
      <p>
        While intoxicated, you exaggerated situations and sometimes assumed
        friends hated you because you'd done something wrong. Sending messages
        briefly made you feel heard, but some messages caused real damage.
        The next morning brought both catastrophic worries and actual repairs
        to make. Your friends ultimately showed that they cared, challenging
        the idea that you had been abandoned.
      </p>

      <div class="ca-grid">
        <div class="ca-box">
          <h3>WHAT YOU EXPECTED</h3>
          <p>Relief from pain, an immediate chance to drink, or the feeling of finally being heard.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ca-box">
          <h3>WHAT YOU EXPERIENCED</h3>
          <p>Temporary relief or satisfaction followed by blackouts, legal consequences, shame, or damaged relationships.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ca-callout">
        THE SHORT-TERM REWARD<br>
        ISN'T THE WHOLE STORY.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 06 / PRACTICE -->
  <section class="ca-section ca-yellow">
    <div class="ca-heading">
      <p class="ca-number">06 / LEARNING THROUGH ACTION</p>
      <h2>TRY IT.<br>NOTICE.<br>LEARN.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ca-copy">
      <p class="ca-lead">
        CBT isn't about replacing every negative thought with a cheerful one.
        It's about developing a more accurate picture and acting on it.
      </p>
      <div class="ca-grid">
        <div class="ca-box">
          <h3>THE PREDICTION</h3>
          <p>Identify the belief: “I can't tolerate this,” or “They don't care about me.”</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ca-box">
          <h3>THE EXPERIMENT</h3>
          <p>Choose a safe alternative when stable: delay an impulsive message, check the facts, or reach out for support.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ca-box">
          <h3>THE OBSERVATION</h3>
          <p>Notice what actually happened, rather than treating the prediction as proof.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ca-box">
          <h3>THE NEXT STEP</h3>
          <p>Adjust your plan based on the evidence. For mania, include psychiatric care and early intervention—not just thought exercises.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="cbt-addiction-ending">
    <div class="ca-ending-heading">
      <p class="ca-ending-small">KEEP ASKING BETTER QUESTIONS.</p>
      <h2>LOOK<br>A LITTLE<br><span>CLOSER.</span></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ca-ending-copy">
      <p>
        Across your stories, alcohol promised relief, quiet, or connection.
        The immediate reward often overshadowed the consequences. CBT helps
        you examine the beliefs and reinforced behaviors behind those decisions
        without denying the effects of chronic pain, mania, or intoxication.
      </p>
      <p>
        The point isn't to excuse what happened or condemn yourself forever.
        It's to recognize the pattern earlier, question the assumptions you can,
        and make decisions based on more than the urgency of the moment.
      </p>
      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION PERSPECTIVES",
          addiction_perspectives_path,
          class: "ca-back-button" %>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>
